[metadata] {"type": "create", "filePath": "/app/output/final_report.html", "content": "<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Global Cost of Living Crisis Index 2026</title>
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js@4.4.7/dist/chart.umd.min.js"></script>
<style>
:root {
  --primary: #1a2744;
  --secondary: #2c5282;
  --accent: #e53e3e;
  --accent2: #dd6b20;
  --accent3: #38a169;
  --bg: #f7fafc;
  --card-bg: #ffffff;
  --text: #2d3748;
  --text-light: #718096;
  --border: #e2e8f0;
  --tier1: #3182ce;
  --tier2: #38a169;
  --tier3: #d69e2e;
  --tier4: #dd6b20;
  --tier5: #e53e3e;
}

* { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }

body {
  font-family: 'Segoe UI', system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;
  background: var(--bg);
  color: var(--text);
  line-height: 1.6;
}

.report-header {
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--primary) 0%, var(--secondary) 100%);
  color: white;
  padding: 3rem 2rem;
  text-align: center;
}

.report-header h1 {
  font-size: 2.2rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  letter-spacing: -0.02em;
}

.report-header .subtitle {
  font-size: 1.1rem;
  opacity: 0.85;
  max-width: 800px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.report-header .meta {
  margin-top: 1rem;
  font-size: 0.85rem;
  opacity: 0.7;
}

.container {
  max-width: 1200px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 0 1.5rem;
}

.kpi-strip {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr);
  gap: 1rem;
  margin: -2rem auto 2rem;
  position: relative;
  z-index: 1;
}

.kpi-card {
  background: var(--card-bg);
  border-radius: 12px;
  padding: 1.25rem;
  box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);
  text-align: center;
  border-top: 3px solid var(--secondary);
}

.kpi-card .kpi-value {
  font-size: 1.8rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--primary);
  margin: 0.25rem 0;
}

.kpi-card .kpi-label {
  font-size: 0.78rem;
  color: var(--text-light);
  font-weight: 500;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.03em;
}

.kpi-card .kpi-desc {
  font-size: 0.72rem;
  color: var(--text-light);
  margin-top: 0.3rem;
}

section {
  margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
}

.section-card {
  background: var(--card-bg);
  border-radius: 12px;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.04);
  padding: 2rem;
  margin-bottom: 2rem;
}

.section-card h2 {
  font-size: 1.4rem;
  color: var(--primary);
  margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  border-bottom: 2px solid var(--secondary);
  padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
}

.section-card h3 {
  font-size: 1.05rem;
  color: var(--secondary);
  margin: 1.5rem 0 0.75rem;
}

.section-card p {
  margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  font-size: 0.92rem;
  color: var(--text);
}

.chart-container {
  position: relative;
  width: 100%;
  margin: 1.5rem 0;
}

.chart-container canvas {
  max-height: 500px;
}

.chart-row {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  gap: 1.5rem;
}

.chart-row .chart-container canvas {
  max-height: 400px;
}

table {
  width: 100%;
  border-collapse: collapse;
  font-size: 0.85rem;
  margin: 1rem 0;
}

thead th {
  background: var(--primary);
  color: white;
  padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
  text-align: left;
  font-weight: 600;
  font-size: 0.78rem;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.03em;
}

tbody td {
  padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
}

tbody tr:hover { background: #f0f4f8; }

.tier-badge {
  display: inline-block;
  padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem;
  border-radius: 4px;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: white;
}

.tier-1 { background: var(--tier1); }
.tier-2 { background: var(--tier2); }
.tier-3 { background: var(--tier3); }
.tier-4 { background: var(--tier4); }
.tier-5 { background: var(--tier5); }

.callout {
  background: #ebf4ff;
  border-left: 4px solid var(--secondary);
  padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
  border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
  margin: 1rem 0;
  font-size: 0.88rem;
}

.callout.warning {
  background: #fff5f5;
  border-left-color: var(--accent);
}

.callout.insight {
  background: #f0fff4;
  border-left-color: var(--accent3);
}

.callout strong { color: var(--primary); }

.heatmap-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(140px, 1fr));
  gap: 0.5rem;
  margin: 1rem 0;
}

.heatmap-cell {
  padding: 0.6rem;
  border-radius: 6px;
  text-align: center;
  font-size: 0.78rem;
  font-weight: 600;
}

.heatmap-cell .city-name { font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 0.15rem; }
.heatmap-cell .city-val { font-size: 1rem; }

.two-col { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem; }

footer {
  text-align: center;
  padding: 2rem;
  color: var(--text-light);
  font-size: 0.8rem;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  margin-top: 2rem;
}

@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .kpi-strip { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  .chart-row, .two-col { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
}

@media (max-width: 600px) {
  .kpi-strip { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
  .report-header h1 { font-size: 1.5rem; }
}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header class="report-header">
  <h1>Global Cost of Living Crisis Index 2026</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A Comparative Analysis of Economic Stress Across Cities and Regions</p>
  <p class="meta">80 Cities &bull; 16 Regions &bull; All indices benchmarked to New York, USA (index = 100) &bull; Data Year: 2026</p>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- KPI STRIP -->
<div class="kpi-strip">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Median Cost of Living</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">65.5</div>
    <div class="kpi-desc">Index (NYC = 100)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg Rent-to-Salary</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">61.5%</div>
    <div class="kpi-desc">Global mean rent burden</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Purchasing Power Gap</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">148.7</div>
    <div class="kpi-desc">Max–Min PPP index spread</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">High-Crisis Cities</div>
    <div class="kpi-value" style="color: var(--accent);">14</div>
    <div class="kpi-desc">Tier 5 (most severe)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg Inflation Rate</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">8.3%</div>
    <div class="kpi-desc">2025 annual average</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW -->
<section>
  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>1. Executive Overview</h2>
    <p>The 2026 Global Cost of Living Crisis Index reveals a starkly bifurcated world. At one extreme, seven cities — five in Switzerland plus Doha and Riyadh — sit in <strong>Tier 1 (Affluent – High Cost / Low Cost – High Wages)</strong>, combining high or moderate living costs with robust purchasing power and inflation rates averaging just 1.4%. At the other, <strong>14 cities now occupy Tier 5</strong>, the most severe crisis classification, where hyperinflation, armed conflict, or deep structural poverty compound already dire affordability conditions.</p>

    <p>The median cost-of-living index across all 80 cities is <strong>65.5</strong> (relative to New York at 100), but this headline figure masks an enormous spread: Zurich leads at 118.5 while Kathmandu sits at just 24.0. More telling is the <strong>purchasing power gap of 148.7 index points</strong> between Basel (183.7) and Beirut (35.0) — a ratio of more than 5:1 in real purchasing capacity.</p>

    <p>Globally, the average rent-to-salary ratio stands at <strong>61.5%</strong>, meaning the typical city-dweller spends well over half their net income on a one-bedroom apartment in the city center. This figure alone signals a systemic housing affordability failure. Meanwhile, the average inflation rate of <strong>8.3%</strong> is heavily skewed by outliers: Buenos Aires (150%), Beirut (50%), and Istanbul (47%) inflate the global mean, while 45 of the 80 cities report inflation below 4%.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> The crisis is not uniformly distributed. It concentrates in cities with a toxic combination of low purchasing power, high inflation, and conflict or sanctions exposure — predominantly in South Asia, Sub-Saharan Africa, the Middle East, and parts of Latin America and Eastern Europe.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 2: REGIONAL COMPARISON -->
<section>
  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>2. Regional Cost-of-Living Comparison</h2>
    <p>Regional averages reveal systematic patterns in how economic stress is distributed geographically. Western Europe and North America dominate in absolute cost of living but also lead in purchasing power, while South Asia and Sub-Saharan Africa report the lowest indices across nearly all dimensions.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="regionBarChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Regional Profiles</h3>
    <table id="regionTable">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Region</th><th>Cities</th><th>Avg COL Index</th><th>Avg Rent Index</th><th>Avg PPP Index</th><th>Avg Salary (USD)</th><th>Avg Rent Burden</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Western Europe</td><td>16</td><td>88.6</td><td>49.6</td><td>141.2</td><td>$4,849</td><td>66.9%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Northern Europe</td><td>5</td><td>85.5</td><td>40.2</td><td>119.4</td><td>$3,984</td><td>68.2%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>North America</td><td>12</td><td>82.2</td><td>65.3</td><td>132.2</td><td>$5,108</td><td>67.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Oceania</td><td>3</td><td>73.7</td><td>49.9</td><td>109.0</td><td>$3,700</td><td>63.9%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>East Asia</td><td>5</td><td>63.0</td><td>39.8</td><td>87.3</td><td>$2,720</td><td>63.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Middle East</td><td>6</td><td>59.1</td><td>32.5</td><td>97.8</td><td>$2,767</td><td>59.4%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Southeast Asia</td><td>5</td><td>51.3</td><td>29.4</td><td>76.3</td><td>$1,692</td><td>57.9%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Eastern Europe</td><td>6</td><td>48.7</td><td>20.8</td><td>76.7</td><td>$1,250</td><td>57.6%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Central Asia</td><td>1</td><td>38.0</td><td>18.0</td><td>72.0</td><td>$750</td><td>55.6%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Western Asia</td><td>1</td><td>38.0</td><td>19.0</td><td>58.0</td><td>$900</td><td>57.1%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Latin America</td><td>5</td><td>38.2</td><td>17.2</td><td>50.2</td><td>$630</td><td>57.6%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Southern Africa</td><td>2</td><td>37.5</td><td>16.5</td><td>53.5</td><td>$1,250</td><td>56.2%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>East Africa</td><td>1</td><td>36.0</td><td>14.0</td><td>55.0</td><td>$450</td><td>53.1%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>West Africa</td><td>2</td><td>35.5</td><td>13.5</td><td>46.0</td><td>$400</td><td>52.8%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>North Africa</td><td>2</td><td>29.0</td><td>9.0</td><td>45.0</td><td>$333</td><td>52.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>South Asia</td><td>8</td><td>28.9</td><td>9.9</td><td>52.5</td><td>$494</td><td>53.6%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Outlier Analysis</h3>

    <div class="callout insight">
      <strong>North America — New York vs. Houston:</strong> Within the same region, New York's cost-of-living index (100.0) is 42% higher than Houston's (70.5), yet Houston's purchasing power index (140.0) exceeds New York's (100.0) by 40%. Houston represents one of the most favorable cost-to-income ratios in the dataset.
    </div>

    <div class="callout insight">
      <strong>Middle East — Beirut vs. Doha:</strong> These two cities belong to the same region but occupy opposite ends of the crisis spectrum. Doha (Tier 1, PPP 145.0, inflation 1.8%) reflects oil-wealth insulation, while Beirut (Tier 5, PPP 35.0, inflation 50.0%) is in the throes of economic collapse. The purchasing power gap within this single region is 110 index points.
    </div>

    <div class="callout insight">
      <strong>Western Europe — Milan vs. Basel:</strong> Milan is classified as Tier 3 with a purchasing power index of just 83.5 — the lowest in the region and far below the Western Europe average of 141.2. Despite similar cost-of-living indices, Milan's average salary ($2,500/mo) is 63% lower than Basel's ($6,800/mo).
    </div>

    <div class="callout insight">
      <strong>Southeast Asia — Singapore vs. Manila:</strong> Singapore (Tier 2, PPP 105.5) operates as a high-cost, high-wage economy; Manila (Tier 5, PPP 52.0) sits at the opposite extreme with severe affordability crisis classification. Singapore's cost-of-living index (87.7) is more than double Manila's (40.0), yet its salary is 10× higher.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: AFFORDABILITY AND RENT BURDEN -->
<section>
  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>3. Affordability and Rent Burden Analysis</h2>
    <p>Housing cost as a proportion of income is the most direct measure of lived economic stress. The <code>rent_to_salary_ratio_pct</code> captures the percentage of average monthly net salary consumed by a one-bedroom city-center apartment. Across all 80 cities, this ratio ranges from <strong>51.0% (Lahore)</strong> to <strong>71.2% (Seattle)</strong> — meaning that even in the "best" case, more than half of income goes to rent.</p>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <strong>No city in the dataset has a rent-to-salary ratio below 50%.</strong> The global median of 61.5% indicates a systemic housing affordability crisis that transcends income levels, geographies, and crisis tiers. Policy interventions focused solely on income growth without addressing housing supply and cost will fail to meaningfully improve affordability.
    </div>

    <h3>Top 15 Cities by Rent Burden</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="rentBurdenChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Rent Burden Across All 80 Cities</h3>
    <div class="chart-container" style="height: 700px;">
      <canvas id="rentBurdenAllChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Salary vs. Rent: The Affordability Frontier</h3>
    <p>The scatter plot below maps each city by its average net salary (x-axis) and city-center rent (y-axis). Points closer to the upper-left represent cities where rent is disproportionately high relative to income — the least affordable. Points in the lower-right are the most affordable.</p>
    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="scatterSalaryRent"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Key Findings</h3>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h3 style="margin-top:0">Highest Rent Burden Cities</h3>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>City</th><th>Rent/Salary</th><th>Salary</th><th>Rent</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Seattle</td><td><strong>71.2%</strong></td><td>$6,200</td><td>$2,107</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Zurich</td><td><strong>70.8%</strong></td><td>$7,200</td><td>$2,471</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Geneva</td><td><strong>70.2%</strong></td><td>$7,984</td><td>$2,279</td></tr>
