<!-- SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS AND RECOMMENDATIONS -->
<section>
  <div class="section-card">
    <h2>6. Key Findings and Policy Implications</h2>

    <h3>Finding 1: Housing Affordability Is a Universal Crisis</h3>
    <p>No city in this dataset achieves a rent-to-salary ratio below 50%. The global mean of 61.5% indicates that the majority of urban workers worldwide are spending more than half their income on housing. This is not confined to low-income cities — Seattle (71.2%), Zurich (70.8%), and Geneva (70.2%) top the list. Housing supply constraints and speculative pricing affect wealthy and poor cities alike, though the underlying mechanisms differ.</p>
    <p><strong>Policy implication:</strong> Housing interventions must be tailored by context. High-income cities need supply-side reforms (zoning, construction); low-income cities need income support, public housing, and infrastructure investment.</p>

    <h3>Finding 2: Inflation Is the Single Strongest Crisis Predictor</h3>
    <p>Average inflation escalates dramatically across tiers: 1.4% (Tier 1) → 3.0% (Tier 2) → 3.4% (Tier 3) → 8.9% (Tier 4) → 28.4% (Tier 5). The jump between Tiers 4 and 5 is especially pronounced, suggesting a threshold effect where inflation above ~10% rapidly degrades affordability and purchasing power. Buenos Aires (150%), Beirut (50%), and Istanbul (47%) represent extreme cases where monetary instability is the primary crisis driver.</p>
    <p><strong>Policy implication:</strong> Inflation stabilization should be treated as the highest-priority macroeconomic intervention for Tier 4 and 5 cities. Central bank independence, fiscal discipline, and exchange rate management are prerequisites for broader affordability improvements.</p>

    <h3>Finding 3: Oil Shock Exposure Amplifies Vulnerability</h3>
    <p>Cities with high oil shock exposure average 20.4% inflation and 67.8 purchasing power index, versus 3.4% inflation and 128.6 PPP for low-exposure cities. The "High" category is heterogeneous — it includes both oil-producing economies (Riyadh, Doha, Dubai) that benefit from price increases and oil-importing developing economies (Lagos, Accra, Dhaka) that are devastated by them.</p>
    <p><strong>Policy implication:</strong> Energy transition and fuel subsidy reform are urgent for oil-importing Tier 4–5 cities. Oil-producing cities should invest surpluses in economic diversification to reduce long-term vulnerability.</p>

    <h3>Finding 4: The Purchasing Power Gap Defines Global Inequality</h3>
    <p>The 148.7-point spread between Basel (183.7) and Beirut (35.0) means a worker in Basel commands over 5× the real purchasing power of one in Beirut. This gap is wider than the cost-of-living spread itself (94.5 points from Zurich to Kathmandu), indicating that income inequality exceeds cost inequality — the core mechanism of the affordability crisis.</p>
    <p><strong>Policy implication:</strong> Cost-of-living indices alone are misleading. Affordability analysis must center on purchasing power and income-relative metrics. International development efforts should prioritize wage growth in low-PPP cities.</p>

    <h3>Finding 5: Regional Averages Mask Critical Within-Region Variation</h3>
    <p>The Middle East encompasses both Doha (Tier 1, PPP 145) and Beirut (Tier 5, PPP 35). Southeast Asia spans Singapore (Tier 2, PPP 105.5) and Manila (Tier 5, PPP 52). Policy interventions designed for regional aggregates will misallocate resources; city-level targeting is essential.</p>
    <p><strong>Policy implication:</strong> International organizations should shift from regional aid frameworks to city-level vulnerability assessments, particularly in regions with high internal dispersion.</p>

